.TH PS 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
ps \- process status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ps
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ps
prints certain indicia about active processes.
The listing is columnar and contains:
.TP
.B PID
The process ID; use this number with kill(1).
.TP
.B S
State: R running or runnable, S blocked, Z exited but not yet reaped,
T stopped.
.TP
.B BANK
The physical auxiliary-memory bank occupied by the process, in hexadecimal.
.TP
.B PPID
The parent process ID.
A process whose parent has exited is adopted by init, so its PPID is 1.
.TP
.B COMMAND
The process's arguments, joined by single spaces and cut to the width of the
console (one column is left free; output that is not a console is cut at 63
characters).
Three markers stand in for arguments:
.TP
.B <defunct>
the process has exited and not yet been reaped (a zombie);
.TP
.B (name)
the program reused the low memory its arguments were staged in, so what is
there no longer reads as an argument list; ps shows the program's name in
parentheses, as BSD does, taken from the kernel's record of what was spawned
into that process's bank, or
.B ?
if the kernel has none;
.TP
.B [name]
the process was made by the kernel, not spawned, so it never had arguments:
.B [init]
for process 1 and
.B [idle]
for the others whose parent is 0.
.PP
Every occupied process slot is shown; unused slots are omitted.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/PS  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
kill(1), sh(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0.
.SH BUGS
There are no selection options, CPU times or Unix ownership fields.
Only the first 64 bytes of the argument strings are read, so a long command line is cut there even on a wide console.
